I am testing open:creib.forge.conformance.records.recover_json_object. The documented rule I am reading says: “The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text,” and also: “Every top-level balanced object in the text is a candidate,” with the further note that “A candidate that parses as strict JSON, or as JSON with repeated keys resolved last-wins, is scoreable; one that does not (a float, for instance) is passed over.”

The two reply texts are as follows. Reply A is exactly the string: ```json\n{"a":1} {"b":2}\n```\n{"c":3}. Reply B is exactly the string: {"c":3}.

For Reply A, the rule requires the answer to be the last object inside the code fence, which is {"b":2}, because the fence holds two top-level objects and the last one inside the fence is {"b":2}. For Reply B, the rule requires the answer to be the last top-level object in the text, which is {"c":3}. The function recover_json_object, however, returns {"c":3} for both replies. In Reply A, the fenced pool takes the entire fenced span {"a":1} {"b":2} as a single candidate; that candidate fails to parse as one JSON object, so the function falls through to the top-level pool, whose last candidate is {"c":3}. Thus the two replies receive the same answer even though the rule requires different answers.

I would be wrong if, on Reply A, recover_json_object returned {"b":2} or any object other than {"c":3}, or if the documented rule were read as requiring the top-level fallback whenever the fenced span as a whole does not parse, rather than requiring the last top-level object inside the fence.